DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The uuggiiddffww utility provides an ipfw(8)-like interface to manage access to
     file system objects by UID and GID, supported by the mac_bsdextended(4)
     mac(9) policy.

     The arguments are as follows:

           aadddd ssuubbjjeecctt _._._. oobbjjeecctt _._._. mmooddee _a_r_s_w_x_n
                   Add a new rule, automatically selecting the rule number.
                   See the description of sseett for syntax information.

           lliisstt    Produces a list of all the current uuggiiddffww rules in the sys‐
                   tem.

           sseett _r_u_l_e_n_u_m ssuubbjjeecctt _._._. oobbjjeecctt _._._. mmooddee _a_r_s_w_x_n
                   Add a new rule or modify an existing rule.  The arguments
                   are as follows:

                   _r_u_l_e_n_u_m  Rule number.  Entries with a lower rule number are
                            applied first; placing the most frequently-matched
                            rules at the beginning of the list (i.e., lower-
                            numbered) will yield a slight performance in‐
                            crease.

                   ssuubbjjeecctt [nnoott] [[!!] uuiidd _u_i_d | _m_i_n_u_i_d_:_m_a_x_u_i_d] [[!!] ggiidd _g_i_d |
                            _m_i_n_g_i_d_:_m_a_x_g_i_d] [[!!] jjaaiilliidd _j_a_i_l_i_d]
                            Subjects performing an operation must match all
                            the conditions given.  A leading nnoott means that
                            the subject should not match the remainder of the
                            specification.  A condition may be prefixed by !!
                            to indicate that particular condition must not
                            match the subject.  The subject can be required to
                            have a particular _u_i_d and/or _g_i_d.  A range of
                            uids/gids can be specified, separated by a colon.
                            The subject can be required to be in a particular
                            jail with the _j_a_i_l_i_d.

                   oobbjjeecctt [nnoott] [[!!] uuiidd _u_i_d | _m_i_n_u_i_d_:_m_a_x_u_i_d] [[!!] ggiidd _g_i_d |
                            _m_i_n_g_i_d_:_m_a_x_g_i_d] [[!!] ffiilleessyyss _p_a_t_h] [[!!] ssuuiidd] [[!!]
                            ssggiidd] [[!!] uuiidd__ooff__ssuubbjjeecctt] [[!!] ggiidd__ooff__ssuubbjjeecctt]
                            [[!!] ttyyppee _a_r_d_b_c_l_s_p]
                            The rule will apply only to objects matching all
                            the specified conditions.  A leading nnoott means
                            that the object should not match all the remaining
                            conditions.  A condition may be prefixed by !! to
                            indicate that particular condition must not match
                            the object.  Objects can be required to be owned
                            by the user and/or group specified by _u_i_d and/or
                            _g_i_d.  A range of uids/gids can be specified, sepa‐
                            rated by a colon.  The object can be required to
                            be in a particular filesystem by specifying the
                            filesystem using ffiilleessyyss.  Note, if the filesystem
                            is unmounted and remounted, then the rule may need
                            to be reapplied to ensure the correct filesystem
                            id is used.  The object can be required to have
                            the ssuuiidd or ssggiidd bits set.  The owner of the ob‐
                            ject can be required to match the uuiidd__ooff__ssuubbjjeecctt
                            or the ggiidd__ooff__ssuubbjjeecctt attempting the operation.
                            The type of the object can be restricted to a sub‐
                            set of the following types.

                                  aa  any file type
                                  rr  a regular file
                                  dd  a directory
                                  bb  a block special device
                                  cc  a character special device
                                  ll  a symbolic link
                                  ss  a unix domain socket
                                  pp  a named pipe (FIFO)

                   mmooddee _a_r_s_w_x_n
                            Similar to chmod(1), each character represents an
                            access mode.  If the rule applies, the specified
                            access permissions are enforced for the object.
                            When a character is specified in the rule, the
                            rule will allow for the operation.  Conversely,
                            not including it will cause the operation to be
                            denied.  The definitions of each character are as
                            follows:

                                  aa  administrative operations
                                  rr  read access
                                  ss  access to file attributes
                                  ww  write access
                                  xx  execute access
                                  nn  none

           rreemmoovvee _r_u_l_e_n_u_m
                   Disable and remove the rule with the specified rule number.

